and shift it to the right.

279
00:09:09,910 --> 00:09:11,250
So shifting it to the right,

280
00:09:11,250 --> 00:09:11,950
because as we know,

281
00:09:11,950 --> 00:09:13,570
So where does this 1 go? It ends up right here.

282
00:09:13,570 --> 00:09:16,010
Think about it — right? Right here.

283
00:09:16,010 --> 00:09:16,470
So what about the 0s on the right here?

284
00:09:16,470 --> 00:09:19,230
They get pushed out.

285
00:09:19,230 --> 00:09:19,830
Nothing much to say about that.

286
00:09:19,830 --> 00:09:21,150
So what's left here?

287
00:09:21,150 --> 00:09:22,430
There were originally 4 zeros,

288
00:09:22,430 --> 00:09:24,070
and now there are only 3 zeros.

289
00:09:24,070 --> 00:09:25,150
Now over here,

290
00:09:25,150 --> 00:09:27,010
these 3 zeros over here,

291
00:09:27,010 --> 00:09:28,290
naturally follow this 1,

292
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:29,490
and shift to the right.

293
00:09:29,490 --> 00:09:30,450
So over here,

294
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:31,390
it's of course 3 zeros.

295
00:09:31,390 --> 00:09:34,980
And here,

296
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:36,200
one bit gets freed up,

297
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:37,820
because after it shifts right,

298
00:09:37,820 --> 00:09:39,580
what we're talking about right now is a right shift,

299
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:41,120
a shift to the right.

300
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:42,940
Shifting to the right.

301
00:09:42,940 --> 00:09:43,900
So with a right shift,

302
00:09:43,900 --> 00:09:44,900
this side needs to be filled in.

303
00:09:44,900 --> 00:09:45,980
That filling in is exactly this concept.

304
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,360
So with a logical shift,

305
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:47,880
note that

306
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:48,920
when filling in,

307
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:51,020
everything is filled with 0.

308
00:09:51,020 --> 00:09:51,860
That's what it means.

309
00:09:51,860 --> 00:09:53,080
This is called a logical shift.

310
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:55,340
And shifting left works the same way.

311
00:09:55,340 --> 00:09:58,860
This is called a logical shift.

312
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:00,240
We use logical shifts all the time.

313
00:10:00,240 --> 00:10:03,300
I wonder if any of you

314
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:04,860
have used, say, C to program,

315
00:10:04,860 --> 00:10:06,140
you'll actually still use this kind of thing.

316
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:08,140
So what do we often use these shifts for?

317
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:10,140
Have you thought about some scenarios?

318
00:10:10,140 --> 00:10:17,990
For example, we often need to

319
00:10:17,990 --> 00:10:20,690
let's take a simple example.

320
00:10:20,690 --> 00:10:23,470
Say I have two 8-bit numbers now.

321
00:10:23,470 --> 00:10:25,350
Now I want to piece it together into a 16-bit number

322
00:10:25,350 --> 00:10:26,050
What do we do? Well, maybe we take the high part of that,

323
00:10:26,050 --> 00:10:28,930
well, maybe we take the high part of that,

324
00:10:28,930 --> 00:10:31,550
Since we need to build that high-order number,

325
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:33,710
we have to shift the low-order number to the left,

326
00:10:33,710 --> 00:10:36,430
shift it left by 8 bits, and it becomes the 8 bits of the high part, right?

327
00:10:36,430 --> 00:10:37,010
Think about it, isn't that right?

328
00:10:37,010 --> 00:10:41,470
This kind of thing is a left shift.

329
00:10:41,470 --> 00:10:42,630
We often right shift,

330
00:10:42,630 --> 00:10:44,190
and we use it in this kind of scenario.

331
00:10:44,190 --> 00:10:45,710
This is logical shift.

332
00:10:45,710 --> 00:10:49,130
Now, opposite to that,

333
00:10:49,130 --> 00:10:51,790
there is something called arithmetic shift.

334
00:10:51,790 --> 00:10:53,930
Arithmetic shift and logical shift

335
00:10:53,930 --> 00:10:54,630
are somewhat different.

336
00:10:54,630 --> 00:10:56,210
First of all, arithmetic shift

337
00:10:56,210 --> 00:10:57,550
only has arithmetic right shift,

338
00:10:57,550 --> 00:10:58,690
there is no arithmetic left shift.

339
00:10:58,690 --> 00:11:00,310
Please note that.

340
00:11:00,310 --> 00:11:02,170
Also, what exactly is arithmetic shift?

341
00:11:02,170 --> 00:11:03,630
Arithmetic shift means that

342
00:11:03,630 --> 00:11:06,210
unlike a logical shift,

343
00:11:06,210 --> 00:11:08,030
which just fills everything with 0 regardless,

344
00:11:08,030 --> 00:11:11,310
it operates based on your sign bit.

345
00:11:11,310 --> 00:11:12,250
Let me give you an example.

346
00:11:12,250 --> 00:11:16,190
Say, for instance, at this position from just now,

347
00:11:16,190 --> 00:11:18,010
this 1,

348
00:11:18,010 --> 00:11:19,190
for this number,

349
00:11:19,190 --> 00:11:21,370
we know where its sign bit is.

350
00:11:21,370 --> 00:11:22,970
The sign bit is usually just

351
00:11:22,970 --> 00:11:25,650
the most significant bit.

352
00:11:25,650 --> 00:11:26,670
Suppose it is 0.

353
00:11:26,670 --> 00:11:27,310
OK.

354
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:28,270
Then if it's a,

355
00:11:28,270 --> 00:11:32,190
just like the arithmetic right shift we saw earlier,

356
00:11:32,190 --> 00:11:35,230
if the most significant bit is 0,

357
00:11:35,230 --> 00:11:36,690
then it will pad with 0.

358
00:11:36,690 --> 00:11:38,770
But suppose what we have here is not,

359
00:11:38,770 --> 00:11:40,150
not 0,

360
00:11:40,150 --> 00:11:41,530
and suppose there is a 1 here instead.

361
00:11:41,530 --> 00:11:43,010
So think about what effect that produces.

362
00:11:43,010 --> 00:11:45,430
What gets filled in here is

363
00:11:45,430 --> 00:11:46,470
no longer 0,

364
00:11:46,470 --> 00:11:46,910
but what?

365
00:11:46,910 --> 00:11:48,570
It's 1.

366
00:11:48,570 --> 00:11:50,010
So actually, after the right shift,

367
00:11:50,010 --> 00:11:51,150
you end up with something like this.

368
00:11:51,150 --> 00:11:52,670
This 1,

369
00:11:52,670 --> 00:11:54,790
this 1,

370
00:11:54,790 --> 00:11:57,350
this 1 actually got shifted over from this side.

371
00:11:57,350 --> 00:11:59,070
Everyone with me?

372
00:11:59,070 --> 00:12:00,930
But how did this 1 get in here?

373
00:12:00,930 --> 00:12:03,610
This is exactly what arithmetic right shift shifts in.

374
00:12:03,610 --> 00:12:04,810
Because during an arithmetic right shift,

375
00:12:04,810 --> 00:12:06,750
it sees that you originally had a 1 over here,

376
00:12:06,750 --> 00:12:08,190
so what I shift in is a 1.

377
00:12:08,190 --> 00:12:09,570
Follow what I mean?

378
00:12:09,570 --> 00:12:10,810
If this side were 0,

379
00:12:10,810 --> 00:12:12,230
then this side would become 0.

380
00:12:12,230 --> 00:12:13,950
That is arithmetic right shift.

381
00:12:13,950 --> 00:12:16,810
It actually comes down to the idea of a sign bit.

382
00:12:16,810 --> 00:12:17,730
That is to say, with arithmetic right shift,

383
00:12:17,730 --> 00:12:20,090
it fills in the bits based on your sign bit.

384
00:12:20,090 --> 00:12:22,870
Now, some of you might ask,

385
00:12:22,870 --> 00:12:23,950
why do we say

386
00:12:23,950 --> 00:12:26,870
here there is only arithmetic...

387
00:12:26,870 --> 00:12:29,570
with arithmetic, why is there only right shift

388
00:12:29,570 --> 00:12:30,510
and no left shift?

389
00:12:30,510 --> 00:12:32,270
Just now, that logical shift,

390
00:12:32,270 --> 00:12:33,370
as you can see, had

391
00:12:33,370 --> 00:12:35,290
both left shift and right shift.

392
00:12:35,290 --> 00:12:36,530
Logical shift.

393
00:12:36,530 --> 00:12:38,930
Arithmetic right shift.

394
00:12:38,930 --> 00:12:42,030
Why is there no left shift?

395
00:12:42,030 --> 00:12:43,810
Think it through.

396
00:12:43,810 --> 00:12:45,430
These things are all very simple.

397
00:12:45,430 --> 00:12:46,610
It's really not that hard.

398
00:12:46,610 --> 00:12:47,150
Think about it.

399
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:49,670
What effect would a left shift have?

400
00:12:49,670 --> 00:12:53,360
It would shift your most significant bit right out.

401
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:54,660
Get what I mean?

402
00:12:54,660 --> 00:12:57,100
If the highest bit gets shifted away,

403
00:12:57,100 --> 00:12:58,060
then as far as it's concerned,

404
00:12:58,060 --> 00:13:00,260
it loses that meaning.

405
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:01,740
The sign bit is gone.

406
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:02,600
Get what I mean?

407
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:05,160
So for arithmetic operations,

408
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:08,080
we would never do a right shift or a left shift,

409
00:13:08,080 --> 00:13:09,020
Pay attention here.

410
00:13:09,020 --> 00:13:13,740
This is the difference between arithmetic shift and logical shift.

411
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:14,560
Pay attention here.

412
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:17,440
So what can arithmetic shift be used for?

413
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:20,320
Well, with arithmetic shift,

414
00:13:20,320 --> 00:13:21,260
for example, let me give you an example.

415
00:13:21,260 --> 00:13:23,140
Say I have a number called negative 4,

416
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:23,900
and I want to divide it by 2.

417
00:13:23,900 --> 00:13:27,380
What method can we use to do that?

418
00:13:27,380 --> 00:13:31,840
Actually, we could use the dumbest way,

419
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:32,920
which is to use the division instruction.

420
00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:34,700
Because we haven't covered the multiplication instruction here.

421
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:35,940
We do have the trigger instruction

422
00:13:35,940 --> 00:13:36,580
See what I mean?

423
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:38,100
There's the DIV trigger instruction

424
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:39,920
We could use the trigger instruction to implement it, of course

425
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:42,740
But we can actually implement it with arithmetic shift

426
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:44,260
Because as we said, shifting to the right

427
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:46,280
is actually equivalent to division

428
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:48,100
Why?

429
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:49,280
Let me give you an example

430
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:51,300
Like we just said,

431
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:57,850
let's keep it short,

432
00:13:57,850 --> 00:13:59,670
suppose this is a number,

433
00:13:59,670 --> 00:14:01,510
4, let's say,

434
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:04,880
the number 4,

435
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:06,100
if we shift it right by one bit,

436
00:14:06,100 --> 00:14:06,800
what does it become?

437
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:07,400
It becomes 2.

438
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:08,780
See that?

439
00:14:08,780 --> 00:14:10,220
Hasn't it just been divided by 2?

440
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:11,580
Now, similarly,

441
00:14:11,580 --> 00:14:12,460
If there's a sign bit,

442
00:14:12,460 --> 00:14:13,200
it will still reach the same

443
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:14,300
so for negative four,

444
00:14:14,300 --> 00:14:14,960
it becomes negative two.

445
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:18,160
Now, logical left shift,

446
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:19,440
logical left shift,

447
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:20,240
actually, I forgot to mention this earlier,

448
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:21,700
logical left shift,

449
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:23,300
can actually also be used

450
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:24,060
as a kind of multiplication.

451
00:14:24,060 --> 00:14:25,540
Left shift is actually equivalent to multiplication.

452
00:14:25,540 --> 00:14:26,740
However,

453
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:27,780
with this logical left shift,

454
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:28,620
when doing multiplication,

455
00:14:28,620 --> 00:14:29,180
everyone should note that

456
00:14:29,180 --> 00:14:30,400
it can actually only support

457
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,300
multiplication of positive numbers.

458
00:14:32,300 --> 00:14:34,120
After left shifting a negative number,

459
00:14:34,120 --> 00:14:35,060
its sign bit gets shifted out,

460
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:35,580
and it's lost.

461
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:36,180
Think about it, right?

462
00:14:36,180 --> 00:14:41,000
So actually, when implementing this complex multiplication,

463
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,260
you can't do it purely through logic operations.

464
00:14:43,260 --> 00:14:44,200
Keep that in mind.

465
00:14:44,200 --> 00:14:46,500
And also,

466
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:49,440
using this shifting approach,

467
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:53,780
compared to using dedicated multiply and divide instructions,

468
00:14:53,780 --> 00:14:55,760
it's actually more efficient.

469
00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:57,680
If you have a lot of these shifting operations,

470
00:14:57,680 --> 00:14:59,840
using shifts is actually better.

471
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:03,540
You can write your own C code for multiplication and division,

472
00:15:03,540 --> 00:15:08,960
then use GCC to generate the machine instructions,

473
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:10,200
and you'll see that the compiler

474
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:12,220
actually performs optimizations.

475
00:15:12,220 --> 00:15:13,360
It will use

476
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:16,860
it will try to use shift instructions instead of these

477
00:15:16,860 --> 00:15:19,140
multiply and divide instructions.

478
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:21,140
Keep that in mind.

479
00:15:21,140 --> 00:15:26,400
So that's the shift instructions.

480
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:27,900
They're used quite a lot.

481
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:30,960
Now, for the related exercises for this,

482
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:32,160
I'll just list them here.

483
00:15:32,160 --> 00:15:34,300
You can work through them on your own when you get back.

484
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:36,400
In class,

485
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:37,620
if we did it this way,

486
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:38,320
doing one and writing one,

487
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:39,000
if we did one and wrote one up,

488
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,240
I reckon it would probably,

489
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:43,340
take a lot of class sessions

490
00:15:43,340 --> 00:15:44,640
just to get through this part.

491
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:46,600
Because assembly is actually,

492
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:48,480
a course in its own right,

493
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:49,300
offered as a dedicated class.

494
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:50,840
What we're really doing here is,

495
00:15:50,840 --> 00:15:51,740
as part of RISC-V,

496
00:15:51,740 --> 00:15:53,360
giving you a quick run-through.

497
00:15:53,360 --> 00:15:58,060
So time is still pretty tight.

498
00:15:58,060 --> 00:16:01,920
Now, this exercise 5.4,

499
00:16:01,920 --> 00:16:03,740
is also meant as homework practice.

500
00:16:03,740 --> 00:16:05,400
It's the one I've highlighted here.

501
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:06,260
If it's the practice exercises,

502
00:16:06,260 --> 00:16:07,600
the practice part,

503
00:16:07,600 --> 00:16:09,500
we actually don't provide the source code.

504
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:11,600
So treat those as exercises to do at home.

505
