Basically, after we finish a chapter,

506
00:16:14,240 --> 00:16:15,300
the related exercises,

507
00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:16,520
you can all go back and work on them.

508
00:16:16,520 --> 00:16:20,270
Now let me see how much time is left.

509
00:16:20,270 --> 00:16:23,650
I'll probably finish the load and store instructions.

510
00:16:23,650 --> 00:16:25,250
That's about all we can cover today.

511
00:16:25,250 --> 00:16:28,330
So besides what we just talked about,

512
00:16:28,330 --> 00:16:30,550
this addition, subtraction, addition, subtraction,

513
00:16:30,550 --> 00:16:32,730
and then logical operations,

514
00:16:32,730 --> 00:16:36,310
AND, OR, NOT, left shift, right shift, arithmetic and logical,

515
00:16:36,310 --> 00:16:38,350
besides these shift operations,

516
00:16:38,350 --> 00:16:41,250
we also have another operation that's performed frequently,

517
00:16:41,250 --> 00:16:43,790
and that's reading and writing to memory.

518
00:16:43,790 --> 00:16:45,090
As we mentioned earlier,

519
00:16:45,090 --> 00:16:47,130
all the ones like addition, subtraction, multiplication, division,

520
00:16:47,130 --> 00:16:47,850
the shift instructions

521
00:16:47,850 --> 00:16:49,790
It can only operate on registers directly.

522
00:16:49,790 --> 00:16:51,230
But what's in the registers,

523
00:16:51,230 --> 00:16:51,950
in general,

524
00:16:51,950 --> 00:16:53,670
we always need to read it in from memory.

525
00:16:53,670 --> 00:16:55,070
Now, the concept of reading,

526
00:16:55,070 --> 00:16:55,610
is called a load.

527
00:16:55,610 --> 00:16:57,050
Note this carefully,

528
00:16:57,050 --> 00:16:58,530
this English terminology,

529
00:16:58,530 --> 00:16:59,470
load and store.

530
00:16:59,470 --> 00:17:01,670
Load actually means bringing something in.

531
00:17:01,670 --> 00:17:02,870
It's loading something in.

532
00:17:02,870 --> 00:17:04,250
From your perspective,

533
00:17:04,250 --> 00:17:05,870
you're looking at it from the CPU's point of view.

534
00:17:05,870 --> 00:17:06,630
Do you understand what I mean?

535
00:17:06,630 --> 00:17:08,410
Store, on the other hand, means storing something out.

536
00:17:08,410 --> 00:17:09,570
It's the concept of sending something out.

537
00:17:09,570 --> 00:17:11,070
So it's a concept of writing.

538
00:17:11,070 --> 00:17:13,430
I've marked this with colors here.

539
00:17:13,430 --> 00:17:14,910
When you look at this diagram,

540
00:17:14,910 --> 00:17:16,350
try to understand these concepts.

541
00:17:16,350 --> 00:17:19,130
To make it easier for everyone to remember this instruction,

542
00:17:19,130 --> 00:17:20,290
the abbreviation,

543
00:17:20,290 --> 00:17:21,310
is L and store.

544
00:17:21,310 --> 00:17:22,330
L and S.

545
00:17:22,330 --> 00:17:26,440
Now, one thing to note,

546
00:17:26,440 --> 00:17:27,220
that is,

547
00:17:27,220 --> 00:17:29,400
taking load as an example,

548
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:30,660
for reading,

549
00:17:30,660 --> 00:17:32,240
and writing is the same,

550
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:34,740
there are actually some variations.

551
00:17:34,740 --> 00:17:35,640
As you can see,

552
00:17:35,640 --> 00:17:36,760
it can

553
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:40,700
it can

554
00:17:40,700 --> 00:17:44,180
read byte by byte,

555
00:17:44,180 --> 00:17:45,800
or it can

556
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:48,320
read two bytes at a time.

557
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:49,520
That is, the meaning of

558
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:52,700
B, H, and W here,

559
00:17:52,700 --> 00:17:54,200
which stand for byte,

560
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:56,420
half word, and word.

561
00:17:56,420 --> 00:17:58,700
We need to have this concept.

562
00:17:58,700 --> 00:18:00,260
byte is a byte

563
00:18:00,260 --> 00:18:03,020
and word is

564
00:18:03,020 --> 00:18:04,480
four bytes

565
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:06,600
half word is

566
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:08,160
half of four bytes

567
00:18:08,160 --> 00:18:08,780
right?

568
00:18:08,780 --> 00:18:10,080
So that's two bytes.

569
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:11,420
And the abbreviations

570
00:18:11,420 --> 00:18:12,800
are B, H, and W.

571
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:14,380
Just understand it this way.

572
00:18:14,380 --> 00:18:15,940
This is L.

573
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:17,460
This is divided into three groups.

574
00:18:17,460 --> 00:18:18,580
At the same time,

575
00:18:18,580 --> 00:18:20,120
for these three groups,

576
00:18:20,120 --> 00:18:21,800
there's also something else, that is,

577
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:22,420
look here,

578
00:18:22,420 --> 00:18:24,100
there are some variations.

579
00:18:24,100 --> 00:18:25,200
For example, there's a U here.

580
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:29,080
So let's take this one as an example.

581
00:18:29,080 --> 00:18:29,980
Let's distinguish this.

582
00:18:29,980 --> 00:18:31,700
What's the idea behind this U versus not having a U?

583
00:18:31,700 --> 00:18:34,140
I mean, if it's...

584
00:18:34,140 --> 00:18:37,140
What's the concept here?

585
00:18:37,140 --> 00:18:38,860
What issue comes up when you want to read the value in?

586
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:42,420
So we know how wide our internal registers are, right?

587
00:18:42,420 --> 00:18:45,420
Our internal registers are 32 bits.

588
00:18:45,420 --> 00:18:46,520
Right?

589
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:49,780
But now, take LB for example.

590
00:18:49,780 --> 00:18:52,960
When you do the read at this point, how much data are you reading?

591
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:54,640
You're reading one byte.

592
00:18:54,640 --> 00:18:57,000
That is to say, your register is four bytes wide.

593
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:58,880
But what you read is just one byte.

594
00:18:58,880 --> 00:19:00,300
So here's the issue.

595
00:19:00,300 --> 00:19:02,220
Once you read that one byte in,

596
00:19:02,220 --> 00:19:03,440
in that register of yours,

597
00:19:03,440 --> 00:19:05,620
what do you put in the remaining three bytes?

598
00:19:05,620 --> 00:19:07,500
You see what I mean?

599
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:09,540
For example, this register here,

600
00:19:09,540 --> 00:19:11,280
it's four bytes wide.

601
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:14,760
What we read in is only one byte.

602
00:19:14,760 --> 00:19:16,280
Now, that byte,

603
00:19:16,280 --> 00:19:17,760
we generally put it in the low bits,

604
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:19,020
and that's pretty much it.

605
00:19:19,020 --> 00:19:20,280
But now there's one issue,

606
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:23,360
which is what to put in the upper three bytes of the register,

607
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:24,360
the question of what goes there.

608
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:26,620
See what I mean?

609
00:19:26,620 --> 00:19:28,560
There are two cases here.

610
00:19:28,560 --> 00:19:30,660
One is called sign extension,

611
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:31,860
and the other is called zero extension.

612
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:33,160
We covered these concepts earlier.

613
00:19:33,160 --> 00:19:33,900
What is sign extension?

614
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:37,320
It means that if the byte you read in here,

615
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:38,820
this byte,

616
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:39,800
has a most significant bit of 1,

617
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:42,520
then what does sign extension mean?

618
00:19:42,520 --> 00:19:44,500
All the upper bits become 1s, right?

619
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:45,600
Still following me?

620
00:19:45,600 --> 00:19:47,040
Extend according to the sign bit.

621
00:19:47,040 --> 00:19:50,440
If it's 0, then everything is 0.

622
00:19:50,440 --> 00:19:51,900
This is called sign extension.

623
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:54,700
But if you add a 'u' here,

624
00:19:54,700 --> 00:19:55,960
then it becomes

625
00:19:55,960 --> 00:19:57,480
actually unsigned extension.

626
00:19:57,480 --> 00:19:59,060
Unsigned extension means zero extension.

627
00:19:59,060 --> 00:19:59,760
Zero extension means that

628
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:01,280
no matter what the preceding byte's

629
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:02,900
most significant bit is, 1 or 0,

630
00:20:02,900 --> 00:20:05,100
the upper bits are all cleared to zero.

631
00:20:05,100 --> 00:20:06,660
That's the effect we achieve.

632
00:20:06,660 --> 00:20:09,000
This actually maps to our C language

633
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:10,420
in a way that is basically like

634
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:11,380
somewhat similar to

635
00:20:11,380 --> 00:20:13,660
the concept of unsigned and signed numbers.

636
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:15,380
Take a moment to let that sink in.

637
00:20:15,380 --> 00:20:18,490
And then,

638
00:20:18,490 --> 00:20:19,870
when it performs the operation,

639
00:20:19,870 --> 00:20:20,910
note that it works like this:

640
00:20:20,910 --> 00:20:21,350
that is to say,

641
00:20:21,350 --> 00:20:23,250
It goes by this rs1

642
00:20:23,250 --> 00:20:25,010
the value in this register

643
00:20:25,010 --> 00:20:26,930
its value

644
00:20:26,930 --> 00:20:31,040
that is to say, since you are going to memory to read

645
00:20:31,040 --> 00:20:32,200
then this memory of yours,

646
00:20:32,200 --> 00:20:33,240
this address in memory,

647
00:20:33,240 --> 00:20:34,940
you definitely have to tell the computer.

648
00:20:34,940 --> 00:20:37,820
So how is this memory address computed?

649
00:20:37,820 --> 00:20:42,860
It is obtained by rs1 plus an immediate value.

650
00:20:42,860 --> 00:20:44,620
Because it uses the I-type format.

651
00:20:44,620 --> 00:20:46,380
You can imagine that since it is I-type,

652
00:20:46,380 --> 00:20:47,980
in this instruction,

653
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:52,600
one part holds a 12-bit immediate value.

654
00:20:52,600 --> 00:20:54,620
This immediate value represents this offset here.

655
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:57,140
So it actually takes the value in this register,

656
00:20:57,140 --> 00:20:58,100
adds this immediate value,

657
00:20:58,100 --> 00:20:59,520
and finally gets an address.

658
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:01,080
Then, based on this address,

659
00:21:01,080 --> 00:21:03,180
it goes to memory to fetch that byte.

660
00:21:03,180 --> 00:21:04,520
That is actually how the process works.

661
00:21:04,520 --> 00:21:05,780
So, everyone,

662
00:21:05,780 --> 00:21:08,480
this shouldn't give you too much trouble.

663
00:21:08,480 --> 00:21:09,680
Now, when we talk about offset addressing,

664
00:21:09,680 --> 00:21:10,660
I've listed a few examples here.

665
00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:11,500
Take a moment to get familiar with them.

666
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:13,460
And at the same time,

667
00:21:13,460 --> 00:21:14,960
also,

668
00:21:14,960 --> 00:21:16,620
notice this address range.

669
00:21:16,620 --> 00:21:17,260
Why is it this one?

670
00:21:17,260 --> 00:21:18,320
It's also because of this immediate value,

671
00:21:18,320 --> 00:21:19,140
which is 12 bits wide.

672
00:21:19,140 --> 00:21:21,980
There's one more thing

673
00:21:21,980 --> 00:21:22,680
I want you to pay attention to,

674
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:23,160
and that is,

675
00:21:23,160 --> 00:21:26,090
there's a question here,

676
00:21:26,090 --> 00:21:26,890
let me ask you,

677
00:21:26,890 --> 00:21:27,690
I want to ask everyone,

678
00:21:27,690 --> 00:21:28,430
just look at this,

679
00:21:28,430 --> 00:21:31,890
LBU, LBU,

680
00:21:31,890 --> 00:21:34,350
LHU, LHU.

681
00:21:34,350 --> 00:21:35,170
But then,

682
00:21:35,170 --> 00:21:39,050
why isn't there something like an LWU?

683
00:21:39,050 --> 00:21:42,840
Think about why.

684
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:50,130
This is actually something we covered earlier,

685
00:21:50,130 --> 00:21:52,190
based on what we explained to you before,

686
00:21:52,190 --> 00:21:55,110
about why we do sign extension and zero extension.

687
00:21:55,110 --> 00:21:57,250
I think everyone should get what I mean.

688
00:21:57,250 --> 00:22:00,790
A register is four bytes,

689
00:22:00,790 --> 00:22:04,330
and if you load a word,

690
00:22:04,330 --> 00:22:05,470
what does that mean?

691
00:22:05,570 --> 00:22:08,290
A word is also four bytes,

692
00:22:08,290 --> 00:22:10,910
so the data in memory,

693
00:22:10,910 --> 00:22:11,390
when loaded in,

694
00:22:11,390 --> 00:22:12,850
exactly fills up one register, right?

695
00:22:12,850 --> 00:22:13,330
Think about it, isn't that the case?

696
00:22:13,330 --> 00:22:14,610
Do we still need to extend?

697
00:22:14,610 --> 00:22:16,070
No extension needed,

698
00:22:16,070 --> 00:22:17,510
naturally.

699
00:22:17,510 --> 00:22:18,110
Right?

700
00:22:18,110 --> 00:22:19,170
So these

701
00:22:19,170 --> 00:22:20,490
As you study,

702
00:22:20,490 --> 00:22:21,350
ask yourself these questions

703
00:22:21,350 --> 00:22:22,470
— a few whys.

704
00:22:22,470 --> 00:22:24,670
It may give us a deeper understanding

705
00:22:24,670 --> 00:22:26,470
of the design of this instruction set.

706
00:22:26,470 --> 00:22:27,030
That is to say,

707
00:22:27,030 --> 00:22:30,590
for LW, there is no LWU,

708
00:22:30,590 --> 00:22:35,620
and the reason is that sign extension isn't needed.

709
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:36,640
So this example,

710
00:22:36,640 --> 00:22:37,960
you can go back and match it up yourselves,

711
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:39,680
and see the effect of this sign extension.

712
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:42,960
Now for writes,

713
00:22:42,960 --> 00:22:44,840
it's relatively simple.

714
00:22:44,840 --> 00:22:48,080
For writes, there are only B, H, and W,

715
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:49,860
with no sign extension.

716
00:22:49,860 --> 00:22:55,140
There's no distinction between unsigned writes and signed writes —

717
00:22:55,140 --> 00:22:58,950
store instructions don't distinguish them.

718
00:22:58,950 --> 00:23:00,670
Now think about why that is.

719
00:23:00,670 --> 00:23:03,690
This is also something worth understanding — why.

720
00:23:03,690 --> 00:23:06,680
Everyone think about why.

721
00:23:06,680 --> 00:23:12,910
Because this is actually the reverse of the process we just talked about.

722
00:23:12,910 --> 00:23:13,650
You know this.

723
00:23:13,650 --> 00:23:16,970
For example, take a byte — one byte.

724
00:23:16,970 --> 00:23:17,930
Think about it.

725
00:23:17,930 --> 00:23:20,050
Inside the register there are 4 bytes.

726
00:23:20,050 --> 00:23:21,890
Outside there is only 1 byte.

727
00:23:21,890 --> 00:23:24,350
Now you want to put this thing out.

728
00:23:24,350 --> 00:23:25,630
This is not reading it in.

729
00:23:25,630 --> 00:23:26,650
Do you understand what I mean?

730
00:23:26,650 --> 00:23:27,950
Since you're putting it out,

731
00:23:27,950 --> 00:23:32,250
the CPU will naturally just fill in its low-order byte

732
00:23:32,250 --> 00:23:34,570
and that's it.

733
00:23:34,570 --> 00:23:36,690
There's no such thing as a concept of extension here at all.

734
00:23:36,690 --> 00:23:37,570
Do you understand what I mean?

735
00:23:37,570 --> 00:23:39,930
So naturally, there is no extension issue.

736
00:23:39,930 --> 00:23:43,070
So when writing out,

737
00:23:43,070 --> 00:23:46,110
you don't need to worry about this sign extension issue.

738
00:23:46,110 --> 00:23:48,370
So when you write instructions,

739
00:23:48,370 --> 00:23:50,790
if you know the reason behind this,

740
00:23:50,790 --> 00:23:52,190
it may help you understand it better.

741
00:23:52,190 --> 00:23:56,030
Now, its instruction format

742
00:23:56,030 --> 00:23:57,250
is a new instruction format

743
00:23:57,250 --> 00:23:57,990
called S-type.

744
00:23:57,990 --> 00:24:00,050
The S here stands for store

745
00:24:00,050 --> 00:24:01,330
— it just means store.

746
00:24:01,330 --> 00:24:06,900
That's where the name comes from.

747
00:24:06,900 --> 00:24:08,060
Now, some of you might wonder,

748
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:11,620
why does this immediate look so odd?

749
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:12,780
Look at this immediate —

750
00:24:12,780 --> 00:24:15,100
when it's actually stored in the instruction,

751
00:24:15,100 --> 00:24:16,380
it's split apart, as you can see.

752
00:24:16,380 --> 00:24:18,800
5 bits are placed here,

753
00:24:18,800 --> 00:24:21,120
and here you have bits 5 to 11,

754
00:24:21,120 --> 00:24:21,980
which is 7 bits.

755
00:24:21,980 --> 00:24:24,600
Why not just put these two parts together?

756
00:24:24,600 --> 00:24:27,480
Well, honestly,

757
00:24:27,480 --> 00:24:29,060
when they designed the spec,

758
00:24:29,060 --> 00:24:29,940
there must have been a reason.

759
00:24:29,940 --> 00:24:33,100
And that reason probably has little to do with software.

760
00:24:33,100 --> 00:24:35,280
It's entirely because, possibly, at the lower level...

761
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:37,420
From a hardware design perspective,

762
00:24:37,420 --> 00:24:39,180
it comes down to a decoding efficiency issue.

763
00:24:39,180 --> 00:24:40,960
As for the reason, we don't need to worry about it.

764
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:42,740
When we write, when we write assembly,

765
00:24:42,740 --> 00:24:45,020
we actually don't pay attention to the specific

766
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:46,680
arrangement inside this.

767
00:24:46,680 --> 00:24:47,860
But when decoding,

768
00:24:47,860 --> 00:24:49,160
when you disassemble,

769
00:24:49,160 --> 00:24:50,240
you might need to consider this issue.

770
00:24:50,240 --> 00:24:54,720
So, as for memory reads and writes,

771
00:24:54,720 --> 00:24:57,680
we'll stop here for today.

772
00:24:57,680 --> 00:25:03,490
Then there are still some conditional branches

773
00:25:03,490 --> 00:25:04,050
and jump instructions.

774
00:25:04,050 --> 00:25:05,630
We probably won't have time to cover those today.

775
00:25:05,630 --> 00:25:07,850
We'll cover them in the next class.

776
00:25:07,850 --> 00:25:10,230
As for the assembly instruction part of the content,

777
00:25:10,230 --> 00:25:12,090
you'll notice that

778
00:25:12,090 --> 00:25:14,450
compared to the earlier material,

779
00:25:14,450 --> 00:25:15,530
it's definitely a bit more dry.

780
00:25:15,530 --> 00:25:18,290
Because especially when doing the exercises,

781
00:25:18,290 --> 00:25:21,090
It's also a bit different from writing in a high-level language.

782
00:25:21,090 --> 00:25:23,310
But because in this course of ours,

783
00:25:23,310 --> 00:25:24,470
in this course,

784
00:25:24,470 --> 00:25:26,670
in the end we'll be writing some cutting-edge stuff,

785
00:25:26,670 --> 00:25:27,510
low-level development.

786
00:25:27,510 --> 00:25:30,550
That means at the very least you need to be able to read it.

787
00:25:30,550 --> 00:25:32,150
You get what I mean, right?

788
00:25:32,150 --> 00:25:33,450
If I give you a piece of assembly,

789
00:25:33,450 --> 00:25:35,190
you should at least be able to roughly understand it,

790
00:25:35,190 --> 00:25:36,790
and then make some small modifications.

791
00:25:36,790 --> 00:25:39,690
That's probably about all we'll ask of you.

792
00:25:39,690 --> 00:25:41,090
Writing large blocks of it,

793
00:25:41,090 --> 00:25:42,430
you won't need to do.

794
00:25:42,430 --> 00:25:43,530
But,

795
00:25:43,530 --> 00:25:44,650
as long as you have the basic concepts down,

796
00:25:44,650 --> 00:25:46,330
and can read it, that's fine.

797
00:25:46,330 --> 00:25:47,230
OK.

798
00:25:47,230 --> 00:25:48,410
So that's it for today's class.

799
00:25:48,410 --> 00:25:48,790
All right?

800
00:25:48,790 --> 00:25:49,550
Thank you, everyone.
